No small problem.

263
00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.490
The way I think about it is on the one hand,

264
00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:25.140
there has been just this explosion of interest

265
00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:26.620
in this lifestyle.

266
00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:28.340
I think in the early days,

267
00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:31.110
there was a sense that, oh, this is a fad.

268
00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:33.310
Like, this is just the latest thing it'll pass

269
00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:36.720
like every other fad diets, south beach diet

270
00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:38.222
and whatever, right.

271
00:12:38.222 --> 00:12:41.300
Clearly history has said, otherwise,

272
00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:42.610
like this is here to stay.

273
00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.160
Every restaurant you go into as plant-based options,

274
00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:50.530
the explosion of meat and dairy analog food products

275
00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:51.363
is unbelievable.

276
00:12:51.363 --> 00:12:54.560
Just go to any grocery store across America and quickly,

277
00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.660
that's expanding across the world

278
00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:00.830
and no longer are we in conversations with people

279
00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:04.536
who say, what is plant-based or what is vegan meat?

280
00:13:04.536 --> 00:13:05.923
People get it.

281
00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:09.490
Let's just pause for a moment and recognize

282
00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:13.093
that that's massive progress yet at the same time,

283
00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:18.070
the consumption of meat globally has never been higher.

284
00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.180
The incidence of chronic disease seems to be on the rise,

285
00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.610
the explosion in diabetes, type two diabetes,

286
00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:26.480
all of these chronic lifestyle ailments

287
00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.100
that are rooted in diet and lifestyle

288
00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.220
continue to metastasize.

289
00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:34.570
So it's like these parallel tracks.

290
00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:36.080
So on the one hand,

291
00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.350
we can celebrate the growth of this movement,

292
00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:39.900
on the other hand,

293
00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.100
there's a despairing aspect of how I look at it, where I go,

294
00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:44.830
is this making any difference?

295
00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:46.930
How are we actually going?

296
00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:48.930
There's other systemic things that we have to talk about

297
00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:50.090
healthcare, et cetera.

298
00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:52.570
We have to create environments that are conducive

299
00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.560
to healthier lifestyles, et cetera.

300
00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:55.540
But how do you think of it?

301
00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:59.203
Like how do you stay optimistic and rooted in the mission?

302
00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:03.810
Well, I would say that I am a,

303
00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.752
the glass is half full, not half empty kind of guy.

304
00:14:07.752 --> 00:14:10.283
And so I'm always looking for the,

305
00:14:12.032 --> 00:14:15.590
I'm very optimistic and I have a lot of hope.

306
00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:18.163
I mean, I have hope for humanity.

307
00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:23.430
I see all the, the good that's out there,

308
00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:26.438
I also see a lot of the bad and the ignorance,

309
00:14:26.438 --> 00:14:30.150
but I think so much of it is just educating people.

310
00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:34.630
And that's one of the things that I am,

311
00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:36.800
I spent my whole career doing.

312
00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.910
I mean, you know my path.

313
00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:41.250
I should probably recap that a little bit

314
00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:43.970
for people because there's a lot of new listeners

315
00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.520
since the last time you were on the show.

316
00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:48.050
I was inspired by my father

317
00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:50.790
and his groundbreaking research at the Cleveland clinic,

318
00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:53.176
going back to 1984.

319
00:14:53.176 --> 00:14:55.680
And as a surgeon,

320
00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.530
he kind of decided to tackle the fact that heart disease

321
00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:04.810
was kind of this making of our own cause

322
00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:07.375
because of what we're putting into our mouths.

323
00:15:07.375 --> 00:15:11.430
And he, and I would say, Dean Ornish

324
00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:14.710
have the most profound evidence-based research on the planet

325
00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:16.810
showing you can actually metabolize away

326
00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:19.770
these plaque formation just by changing what you eat.

327
00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:23.410
And so my father has been like you said,

328
00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:25.130
I was a bit of a lighthouse for you.

329
00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.030
My father has been a huge right.

330
00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:30.080
And I don't think it can be overstated,

331
00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.500
just how radical a notion that was

332
00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:36.500
at the time that he was making those proclamations.

333
00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.900
Well, and everybody called him a clack.

334
00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:40.163
They made fun of him,

335
00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:44.840
but my dad has always been a true seeker

336
00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.600
and he doesn't care what people think.

337
00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.720
And so he's got a pretty thick skin.

338
00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:53.022
And so he forged forward and lo and behold,

339
00:15:53.022 --> 00:15:57.130
he started in 1984 and by 1991,

340
00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:01.110
he had some pretty profound angiographic evidence.

341
00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:05.070
Basically, it's like a an x-ray of the arteries

342
00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:06.240
where they've put a dye in there,

343
00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.610
but you can actually see the before and after.

344
00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:10.950
And it was hugely dramatic.

345
00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.920
And he's gone on since then to just do this again and again,

346
00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.710
and again, and again, to show that it wasn't a fluke.

347
00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:20.667
His book came out in 2007,

348
00:16:20.667 --> 00:16:22.930
"Prevent and Reverse Heart Disease".

349
00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:25.030
He's been written up in at least seven

350
00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:29.491
of the peer reviewed medical literatures that are out there.

351
00:16:29.491 --> 00:16:34.060
And he now is sits on the American college of cardiology.

352
00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:35.600
He's one of the members there.

353
00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:37.890
And how old is he now?

354
00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.470
He's gonna be 88 in December.

355
00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:44.880
Just a whippersnapper and as vital as ever.

356
00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:46.542
Has he lost a step at all, or?

357
00:16:46.542 --> 00:16:48.850
No, and he bikes every morning

358
00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.725
and he's doing fantastically well.

359
00:16:51.725 --> 00:16:55.860
Because of COVID, he's a little bit more sheltered in place

360
00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:59.585
because of his age, he's taking some precautionary measures,

361
00:16:59.585 --> 00:17:04.585
but what he was able to do influenced my trajectory,

362
00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:07.680
my whole path in life.

363
00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:11.940
And so when I went to University of Texas,

364
00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:16.940
and as soon as I graduated in 1980, December 86,

365
00:17:17.031 --> 00:17:19.170
I started eating this way.

366
00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:22.730
As soon as I was off the athletic dining room table

367
00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:25.370
with the football players of steaks and bacon and eggs

368
00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:26.840
and all that stuff.

369
00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:28.920
Did you do that kicking and screaming,

370
00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.736
or you were compelled by your father's example?

371
00:17:31.736 --> 00:17:33.210
People always ask that

372
00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:37.271
and now I have so much respect and admiration for my father

373
00:17:37.271 --> 00:17:41.220
and I have always been like one of his biggest cheerleaders.

374
00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:45.020
And so kicking and screaming, not in the least,

375
00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:47.890
I embraced in a big, powerful way.

376
00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:49.980
And I was trying to figure it out,

377
00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:53.560
of course, for the first couple of months, year.

378
00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:55.670
But I also decided when I graduated

379
00:17:55.670 --> 00:18:00.670
that I was not destined for 9:00 to 5:00 desk job.

380
00:18:00.757 --> 00:18:05.757
And it was just made me wanna like shrivel up and die.

381
00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:10.440
So I always loved competition and the thrill of it

382
00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:13.010
and just the black and whiteness of it,

383
00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:15.300
like, okay there's no BS.

384
00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:17.738
It's just whoever crossed the finish line first,

385
00:18:17.738 --> 00:18:18.770
there you go.

386
00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:20.240
You're the winner.

387
00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:23.600
And so I decided to try my hand and triathlons,

388
00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:26.110
and I did that for a decade, right?

389
00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:27.870
Swimming, biking, running.

390
00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.900
I became the premier swimmer in the sport

391
00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:31.930
for almost a decade.

392
00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.840
And one of the top 10 in the United States

393
00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:36.870
at the international distance,

394
00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:38.100
not the Ironman,

395
00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:42.421
that required such a whole nother mentality

396
00:18:42.421 --> 00:18:46.130
and level of, I'm gonna say commitment to training.

397
00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:49.970
And I already was training two to five hours a day

398
00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.960
and I saw, the Molina's of the world

399
00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.100
and Dave Scott and Mark Allen.

400
00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:58.080
And I was like, ah, I just,

401
00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:00.060
I'm gonna stick to the shorter stuff.

402
00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:01.900
But you did compete at Kona

403
00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:04.130
and were first out of the water.

404
00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:05.440
Listen--
You might've gotten past

405
00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:08.723
quickly thereafter, but you got that TV.

406
00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:14.030
I competed in Kona just because you have to,

407
00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:16.722
I mean, back then, I wanted to do it,

408
00:19:16.722 --> 00:19:19.480
it was the granddaddy of all Ironmans.

409
00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:20.450
It's the reason I got into the sport.

410
00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:25.450
I saw Dave Scott in 1981 or two on wide world of sports.

411
00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:28.640
And I was like, oh my God, this guy is a stallion.

412
00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:31.190
I wanna do this.

413
00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:33.920
And I did it. I was first out of the water.

414
00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:37.090
I led until like almost a turnaround at heavy

415
00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:39.810
and then got past that--

416
00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:42.190
So you lead for the first half of the bike.

417
00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:43.820
Oh yeah.
Wow.

418
00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:46.385
And then I was passed by, that year,

419
00:19:46.385 --> 00:19:49.167
it was Dave Scott, then Greg Welch, Ken Glah.

420
00:19:49.167 --> 00:19:53.262
And then I came off to like 200 in like 24.

421
00:19:53.262 --> 00:19:55.100
The monkey jumped on,

422
00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.000
I shouldn't say the piano jumped on my back.

423
00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:00.550
And then I finished like a thousand, like 54.

424
00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:02.990
I mean, it was painful just being vertical.

425
00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:05.310
I mean, I walked a mile, ran a mile.

426
00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:06.550
I was like, oh my gosh.

427
00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:10.030
And so my two to three hour training days

428
00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:13.170
and having one or done one or 200 mile rides

429
00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:16.110
followed by a 10 mile run, didn't do it.

430
00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:17.800
Not the best prep for that race.

431
00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.350
No, no, it didn't matter what I was eating.

432
00:20:21.725 --> 00:20:25.490
So you were pro triathlete for 10 years?

433
00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:26.500
10 Years exactly.

434
00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:30.790
But then I continue to compete at a world-class level,

435
00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:33.530
but not in so much the road triathlons,

436
00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:35.210
but in the off-road triathlon.

437
00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:37.100
So the XTERRA.
The XTERRA races

438
00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:39.360
which I just gobbled up and I loved it.

439
00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:42.710
And it was so different than the road triathlons

440
00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:44.030
where you just kinda put your head down

441
00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:45.763
and just crank that out.

442
00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.060
Mountain biking, you've gotta be like on your game.

443
00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:51.570
There's just so much going on.

444
00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:54.650
I love the off-road running the trail running.

445
00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:58.360
I love the loop course of the swim.

446
00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:00.240
So all that.

447
00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:04.170
So I did every one of the XTERRA World Championships on Maui

448
00:21:05.645 --> 00:21:08.590
from 1999 to almost 2006.

449
00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:11.420
And quietly plant-based the whole time

450
00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.448
before it was a thing.

451
00:21:13.448 --> 00:21:16.017
Yup. For sure.
I mean,

452
00:21:16.017 --> 00:21:18.860
you had to be the only athlete.

453
00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:20.315
Well, keep in mind,

454
00:21:20.315 --> 00:21:23.920
and I don't know what's happened to Dave lately,

455
00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:28.450
but Dave, Scott inspired me because he was 100% vegetarian

456
00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:29.720
and he was so hardcore.

457
00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:32.233
He'd wash his cottage cheese and things like that

458
00:21:32.233 --> 00:21:34.523
to rinse off the excessive fat.

459
00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:38.870
Changed his tune, lately.

460
00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:39.703
Yeah.

461
00:21:39.703 --> 00:21:41.400
I haven't talked to Dave about that, but--

462
00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:44.750
But while he was in top form that was his approach.

463
00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:48.460
And he was vocal about it at the time, as I recall.

464
00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:49.990
Very vocal.

465
00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:50.823
Yeah.
Yeah.

466
00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:55.770
So in, let's see, 1997, I was like,

467
00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:58.960
okay, I've been gallivanting around the globe,

468
00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:00.580
long enough swimming, biking, and running,

469
00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:01.950
It's time for me to hang up the Speedo,

470
00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:05.644
hang up the, the Schwinn bike and the Nike shoes

471
00:22:05.644 --> 00:22:10.563
and that's when I decided to try my hand at firefighting.

472
00:22:10.563 --> 00:22:13.640
And I just love the fact that,

473
00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:16.670
'cause triathlons, it's kind of lonely.

474
00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:18.920
It's such an individual sport.

475
00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:21.691
And I loved the fact that as a firefighter,

476
00:22:21.691 --> 00:22:25.220
I'm part of a crew, I'm part of a team.

477
00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:26.940
This is my second family.

478
00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.130
We're going out and we are doing,

479
00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:31.600
we're helping people, we're saving lives.

480
00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:33.880
And we're doing that like 10 to 15 times a shift.

481
00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:36.740
And I love the fact that you never knew

482
00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:38.510
when that tone went off

483
00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:42.120
what exactly you were gonna find when you got there?

484
00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:43.020
Whether it was a fire,

485
00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:45.036
whether it was somebody that had,

486
00:22:45.036 --> 00:22:46.970
heaven forbid a heart attack,

487
00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:51.162
whether it was a gnarly car accident, investigate smoke,

488
00:22:51.162 --> 00:22:55.880
just gunshot whatever.

489
00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:56.713
I would imagine

490
00:22:56.713 --> 00:23:00.640
that there were more heart attack type visits

491
00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.610
than there were houses on fire.

492
00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:07.360
And that probably was, I suspect like impactful

493
00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:11.990
to see people suffering up close and personal in that way.

494
00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:12.823
Yeah.

495
00:23:12.823 --> 00:23:16.473
Especially given kind of my knowledge and my education,

496
00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:18.620
seeing all these people,

497
00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:23.140
because I'd say 75 to 80% of our call volume

498
00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:26.603
was responding as emergency first responders,

499
00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:33.560
paramedics to this absolute, just dearth of medical calls.

500
00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:40.390
So lifting assistance, calls, chest pain, heart attacks,

501
00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:44.190
people whose blood sugars have gotten too low

502
00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:48.420
and they're on the verge of going into a diabetic coma

503
00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:53.420
and yeah, 80% were all related to,

504
00:23:54.916 --> 00:23:58.130
which I will say is just the standard American diet.

505
00:23:58.130 --> 00:23:59.310
And it was super sad,

506
00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:02.050
'cause I felt like taking every one of them aside

507
00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:05.280
and saying, "Hey, I got the answer for you here."

508
00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.810
Well the big inflection point for you

509
00:24:08.810 --> 00:24:10.600
amidst the firefighting career

510
00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:14.380
was this experiment that you ran with your crew, right?

511
00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:16.070
This competitive group of dudes

512
00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:18.680
who are all trying to best each other in various ways,

513
00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:22.180
you kind of flip the script on that and challenge everybody

514
00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:25.010
to see who could lower their cholesterol the most

515
00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:27.150
in a given set time period.

516
00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:31.150
Exactly. Firefighters are very competitive.

517
00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:32.900
And so we always have these little bets

518
00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:36.270
to see who can make the most free throws

519
00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.400
on the basketball court, in the parking lot,

520
00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:42.100
who can do the most pull-ups push-ups, ping pong games.

521
00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:42.933
So we had a little bit

522
00:24:42.933 --> 00:24:45.060
to see who had the lowest cholesterol level.

523
00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.010
And we found out that one of our,

524
00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:51.350
my firefighting brothers had a cholesterol at 33 of 344.

525
00:24:51.350 --> 00:24:54.210
And so that was really the inflection point

526
00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:57.710
when we as a crew decided we would get behind

527
00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:00.760
this firefighting brother and eat this way

528
00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:03.660
basically in an act of solidarity to help this guy out.

529
00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:08.660
And so this was in 2003 and I challenged this one guy JR

530
00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:14.090
to do it, not only at the firehouse, but also at home.

531
00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:18.730
So he was doing it 24/7 and then within 28 days,

532
00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:20.920
his cholesterol went down to 196.

533
00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:24.000
So 148 points.
Pretty dramatic.

534
00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:27.350
And that's in less than a month, lost 14 pounds

535
00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:30.090
sleeping, better acid reflux went away

536
00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:32.308
the standard protocol.

537
00:25:32.308 --> 00:25:37.308
And then the next thing this is becoming a tradition

538
00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:40.249
at fire station two on the C shift,

539
00:25:40.249 --> 00:25:43.820
the Austin American-Statesman does an article about us.

540
00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.046
Then the New York Times,

541
00:25:46.046 --> 00:25:49.030
the crown jewel of written media

542
00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.370
does a front page article on the Metro section.

543
00:25:51.370 --> 00:25:54.960
And then I'm getting blasted by literary agents

544
00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.100
and publishing houses to write a book

545
00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.870
about the escapades and the adventures

546
00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.110
that we were having at fire station two

547
00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:05.710
where we were doing something as crazy in the land of beef

548
00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:08.970
as eating tofu and broccoli and steel cutouts,

549
00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.460
and sweet potatoes and all that jazz.

550
00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:14.670
Did you have a sense, like a light bulb moment

551
00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:18.890
of like this is opening a door to a new path for me?

552
00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:20.640
Or were you just kind of responding

553
00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:23.360
to what was getting thrown at you at the time?

554
00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:24.688
No, forever.

555
00:26:24.688 --> 00:26:26.803
I was just responding to what was being thrown.

556
00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:31.460
I tell this story, I don't know if I've told you,

557
00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:36.460
but I probably got letters and emails and phone calls

558
00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:42.590
from 30 different publishing houses and literary agents

559
00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:44.950
soliciting me to write a book

560
00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:46.960
and I never responded to any of 'em

561
00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.660
'cause I was like, I'm like, nah, who am I?

562
00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:55.590
I'm not a doctor, I'm not a life coach, I'm not a dietician.

563
00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:58.080
I've got no business writing a book.

564
00:26:58.080 --> 00:26:59.590
And frankly, the thought of writing a book

565
00:26:59.590 --> 00:27:01.340
was very intimidating.

566
00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.300
It's scary.
Super scary.

567
00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:08.300
And at some point, and I'd say it's probably six months

568
00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:13.414
after the barrage started coming in,

569
00:27:13.414 --> 00:27:18.414
I just was like, thinking, you know what, well, why not?

570
00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:23.341
And maybe I can reach a segment of the population

571
00:27:23.341 --> 00:27:25.663
that wouldn't otherwise hear this message

572
00:27:25.663 --> 00:27:27.940
from a doctor or a dietician

573
00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:32.638
or your typical kind of authority figure.

574
00:27:32.638 --> 00:27:34.550
And so I flew to New York

575
00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:38.630
and met with all these literary agents, Richard pine,

576
00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.950
who we've talked about

577
00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:43.510
and who was behind the "The South Beach Diet"

578
00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:46.910
and oh God, a litany of other books

579
00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:49.260
and I decided to go with him as my literary agent.

580
00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.740
And I spent almost two years writing the book,

581
00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:54.790
doing a pilot study and all that.

582
00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:57.580
I know there was some debate or discussion

583
00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:00.725
like this pressure to put the word diet on the cover,

584
00:28:00.725 --> 00:28:02.850
That you were resistant to.

585
00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:05.462
Yeah, I didn't want to, but they insisted,

586
00:28:05.462 --> 00:28:09.650
beyond there, they also insisted that in the recipe section,

587
00:28:09.650 --> 00:28:11.400
you also have to have fish and chicken

588
00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:12.950
as an option for people

589
00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:15.400
because in 2009, when I wrote this,

590
00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:16.740
they're like this is not gonna sell

591
00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.053
for 100% vegan.
So interesting.

592
00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:22.860
As you know now in subsequent additions

593
00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:27.509
of the "Engine 2 Diet" we have removed all the--

594
00:28:27.509 --> 00:28:31.030
So the original edition, have those in there.

595
00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:34.350
For people that wanted it as an option, we had some,

596
00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.547
but it was only a couple of the recipes,

597
00:28:36.547 --> 00:28:39.418
but I felt tainted doing it.

598
00:28:39.418 --> 00:28:40.870
(Rip laughs)

599
00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:42.310
But the book goes on

600
00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:44.440
to become a New York times bestseller,

601
00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:47.780
which had to be surreal for you.

602
00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:51.430
And it really does like create this opportunity

603
00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:55.800
and this platform to step into this sort of call to service

604
00:28:55.800 --> 00:29:00.390
in terms of like, okay, I now have this situation

605
00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:03.080
in which I can really apply what I've learned

606
00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:06.170
to help the many that are suffering in various ways.

607
00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:08.340
And what's so beautiful about what unfolded

608
00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.410
and we talked about this last time

609
00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:14.860
is that it becomes this extraordinary family story.

610
00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:19.570
Like everybody in your family participates in what was,

611
00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:24.570
hence created to basically convey all of this wisdom

612
00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:26.340
from your father and your mother

613
00:29:26.340 --> 00:29:28.840
and your siblings, et cetera,

614
00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:32.163
that has gone on to impact like millions of people.

615
00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:38.190
This has been such a gift right across the board.

616
00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:42.843
And I just feel so blessed and lucky that,

617
00:29:44.291 --> 00:29:47.210
what I've tried to do in my life

618
00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:49.873
is just always follow what I'm passionate about.

619
00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:55.420
And that is kind of served me very well.

620
00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:57.117
So when everybody told me,

621
00:29:57.117 --> 00:29:58.980
"Oh my God, you need to get a real job.

622
00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:02.520
Well, you can't just do triathlons, that's ridiculous."

623
00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:05.510
I was like, listen, just like, let me figure it out.

624
00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:07.040
And I did that for a decade.

625
00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:09.570
And then I became a firefighter

626
00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:12.090
and loved being a firefighter.

627
00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.660
But then after I wrote this book,

628
00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:18.483
the "Engine 2 Diet", and we haven't talked about this yet,

629
00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:23.720
the universe kind of opened up, the clouds parted

630
00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:25.470
and I got this invitation

631
00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:29.460
to become a healthy eating kind of Crusader of sorts

632
00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:31.310
for Whole Food Market stores.

633
00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:35.110
And John Mackey, he read the book and he was like,

634
00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:37.120
Rip, I want you to come on board.

635
00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:41.040
I want you to educate our 100,000 team member base.

636
00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:46.040
I want you to lecture to as many of our customers

637
00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:49.210
as you can, go around to the UK,

638
00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:50.820
Canada and the United States,

639
00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:54.964
visit all of our stores and do that.

640
00:30:54.964 --> 00:30:57.299
I also would love for you to be

641
00:30:57.299 --> 00:31:00.350
a healthy eating immersion partner

642
00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:04.920
and we will pay for our sickest team members to go and learn

643
00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:08.790
from soup to nuts about this lifestyle.

644
00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:13.790
And so, it's just crazy the growth trajectory

645
00:31:14.590 --> 00:31:19.590
that I was thrust into in the span of writing the book,

646
00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:24.970
having it hit February of 2009

647
00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:26.720
and then getting this invitation

648
00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:29.060
to be a healthy eating partner with Whole Foods market

649
00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.520
in really the summer of 2009.

650
00:31:31.520 --> 00:31:35.000
And then I retired from firefighting after 12 years.

651
00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:36.400
Almost reluctantly.

652
00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:38.800
Very, very much so.

653
00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:41.940
But as John Mackey said, he's like "Rip,

654
00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:45.610
if you wanna take helping people and saving lives

655
00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:49.280
to a whole nother level, and I know it's not easy,

656
00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:52.150
but you gotta burn the ships, burn the ships,

657
00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:56.350
leave that career behind and come on board here."

658
00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:57.360
And not for nothing

659
00:31:57.360 --> 00:32:00.530
but I remember back in those early days,

660
00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:03.880
there were certain prominent figures in the movement

661
00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:07.990
who will remain on named, who were of the opinion that

662
00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:10.320
it was impossible to make a living

663
00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:12.360
in this kind of vein of advocacy.

664
00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:13.890
Like you can write a book that's fine.

665
00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:16.290
Or you can try to help people,

666
00:32:16.290 --> 00:32:19.187
but extensively you're gonna need another job.

667
00:32:19.187 --> 00:32:22.110
Because there was no roadmap or blueprint

668
00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:24.430
for what you created.

669
00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:25.263
No.

670
00:32:27.730 --> 00:32:29.800
Well, I can tell you this,

671
00:32:29.800 --> 00:32:32.810
that I wasn't gonna jump from firefighting

672
00:32:32.810 --> 00:32:36.130
unless I had something that was a little bit more solid.

673
00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:39.180
And what John Mackey offered me at Whole Foods

674
00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:42.247
was more than I was making as a firefighter.

675
00:32:42.247 --> 00:32:43.430
It was pretty secure.

676
00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:44.830
So you ended up going on tour.

677
00:32:44.830 --> 00:32:47.890
I mean, he must have done thousands of these presentations

678
00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:50.140
at all these whole food markets all over the place.

679
00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:52.420
Well, I went around for a decade

680
00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:57.420
and I probably did close to 200 a year.

681
00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:01.490
But you know what,

682
00:33:01.490 --> 00:33:04.720
every time that I would get in front of that audience,

683
00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:08.343
whether it was three people or 300,

684
00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:10.710
I gave him everything I had.

685
00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:14.755
Because they were there, they wanted to be educated

686
00:33:14.755 --> 00:33:19.350
and the opportunity to help somebody out

687
00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:21.650
and flip them from the standard American diet

688
00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:24.770
to eating a whole food plant, strong diet,

689
00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:28.070
and I've gotten so many thank yous.

690
00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:30.530
And just weather, and you know this,

691
00:33:30.530 --> 00:33:31.940
but when I'm doing book signings,

692
00:33:31.940 --> 00:33:34.330
like thank you, you saved my life.

693
00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:35.960
Thank you, you and your father saved my life.

694
00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:38.020
You and your family saved my life.

695
00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:40.500
And then as you know,

696
00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:43.747
by John Mackey giving me this opportunity,

697
00:33:43.747 --> 00:33:45.960
I also, in addition to running

698
00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:47.920
these healthy eating immersion programs

699
00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:49.230
that we were doing for seven days,

700
00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.240
we then opened them up to the public.

701
00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:53.271
And then it also gave me the confidence

702
00:33:53.271 --> 00:33:57.440
to start doing these weekend events, which we had you too.

703
00:33:57.440 --> 00:33:58.500
And so--
The plant stock

704
00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:00.910
and the various retreats and immersions and--

705
00:34:00.910 --> 00:34:02.681
They've morphed,

706
00:34:02.681 --> 00:34:05.380
they started out as kind of farms to forks

707
00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:08.160
and then we decided to call it plant stock.

708
00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:11.420
And we had those weekend weekend events

709
00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:13.670
and I think you came to three or four of them

710
00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:14.503
over the course of (indistinct) years.

711
00:34:14.503 --> 00:34:15.676
Yeah they're incredible.

712
00:34:15.676 --> 00:34:16.796
They're incredible.

713
00:34:16.796 --> 00:34:20.330
So back to this point of view of like traveling all over

714
00:34:20.330 --> 00:34:22.275
and doing 200 of these things a year,

715
00:34:22.275 --> 00:34:24.010
beyond the fact that it helped you

716
00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:26.170
become this extraordinary public speaker,

717
00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:29.280
'cause you were so honed in on your message,

718
00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:31.590
other than like the thank you's and et cetera,

719
00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:33.840
what are some of the things you learned

720
00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:37.780
about how to impact people in the most meaningful way?

721
00:34:37.780 --> 00:34:39.500
Like, I'm sure themes, recur,

722
00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:41.910
I know when I see these people,

723
00:34:41.910 --> 00:34:44.490
this is kind of the basic story that they tell,

724
00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:45.820
how do you help them?

725
00:34:45.820 --> 00:34:47.560
And like, what are the things that you learned

726
00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:49.240
from meeting so many people

727
00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:51.140
who were struggling with their health?

728
00:34:54.640 --> 00:34:56.530
I think that the thing that I learned

729
00:34:56.530 --> 00:35:00.980
more than anything was nobody is too far gone.

730
00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:04.240
And that just about anybody can turn it around.

731
00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:07.370
And Dan Buettner talks about this a ton,

732
00:35:07.370 --> 00:35:10.550
but you have to create the right environment

733
00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:12.820
so the healthy choice is the easy choice,

734
00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:14.730
that's a big one.

735
00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:16.890
And you gotta get rid of every one of the excuses

736
00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:18.520
that you're gonna throw out in front of yourself,

737
00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:20.920
because everybody thinks that their excuse

738
00:35:20.920 --> 00:35:22.610
is better than anybody else's.

739
00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:26.330
Whether it's the mother that has seven children

740
00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:28.080
and is she just going nuts?

741
00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:33.080
Whether it's the CEO that is on the road 150 days a year,

742
00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:37.190
whether it's the guy that works off shore on an oil

743
00:35:39.319 --> 00:35:44.319
and doesn't have access to food or an 18 wheel truck driver.

744
00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:46.080
And I say every one of those,

745
00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:49.650
because I met every one of those on my journey

746
00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:51.470
and every one of them was able to figure out ways

747
00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:53.010
of conquering it.

748
00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:53.843
You don't know man,

749
00:35:53.843 --> 00:35:55.550
I understand what you're saying Rip, that's great.

750
00:35:55.550 --> 00:35:58.000
You don't understand my specific problem,

751
00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.930
my life's too complicated for this,

752
00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:01.070
thanks but no thanks.

753
00:36:01.070 --> 00:36:02.040
Yep.

754
00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:04.700
And so we just got to like clear the slate and say,

755
00:36:04.700 --> 00:36:08.370
listen, everybody thinks that, but you can do this,

756
00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:09.490
I believe in you.

757
00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:12.200
I absolutely know that you can do this

758
00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:13.790
and let's give it a try.

759
00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:16.150
And so that's the whole thing with the 28 days

760
00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:19.690
or the seven days or whatever take it for a test drive

761
00:36:19.690 --> 00:36:22.100
and then you'll be the judge after that

762
00:36:22.100 --> 00:36:24.540
is it something that you feel like, was it worth it?

763
00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:25.810
How hard was it?

764
00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:29.630
And then we know that you need support

765
00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:32.710
and you need community, so you don't feel like you're alone,

766
00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:36.470
especially over the, well, when I started this in 2010,

767
00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:39.140
there were hardly anybody that was doing this.

768
00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:41.960
So we've created some really powerful communities

769
00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:44.500
at PLANTSTRONG where people can feel connected

770
00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:45.333
to like-minded people.

771
00:36:45.333 --> 00:36:48.740
And we have coaches that are helping answer questions,

772
00:36:48.740 --> 00:36:52.030
but it's become kind of this community

773
00:36:52.030 --> 00:36:53.880
where everybody helps everybody else.

774
00:36:55.538 --> 00:36:57.420
And the original kind of portal for this

775
00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:00.763
was enginetwo.com that's now evolved into plantstrong.com,

776
00:37:01.770 --> 00:37:04.200
but it's been cool to watch over the years

777
00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:07.360
how you're constantly iterating on the website

778
00:37:07.360 --> 00:37:10.784
and it just becomes a more and more robust

779
00:37:10.784 --> 00:37:15.510
360 degree destination for all the resources that you need

780
00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:18.590
from meal planning to recipe prep

781
00:37:18.590 --> 00:37:22.190
