who got the Appalachian trail record

2296
01:49:33.430 --> 01:49:37.100
and then just recently he went forward again,

2297
01:49:37.100 --> 01:49:39.760
had an issue with his knee and had to drop out,

2298
01:49:39.760 --> 01:49:42.543
but I'm like, wow, Scott,

2299
01:49:45.358 --> 01:49:46.740
'cause I read his book "North"

2300
01:49:46.740 --> 01:49:50.580
and he basically, by the end of that,

2301
01:49:50.580 --> 01:49:53.950
he was mincemeat ,he was absolute mincemeat.

2302
01:49:53.950 --> 01:49:55.610
I mean, he'd lost so much weight.

2303
01:49:55.610 --> 01:49:59.980
He looked like another day that might've done a in him.

2304
01:49:59.980 --> 01:50:02.730
I mean, it was unbelievable and he achieved.

2305
01:50:02.730 --> 01:50:03.660
Unbelievable.

2306
01:50:03.660 --> 01:50:07.560
But again, I applaud people that are getting out there,

2307
01:50:07.560 --> 01:50:10.010
getting after it pushing the boundaries.

2308
01:50:10.010 --> 01:50:11.563
I mean, wow.

2309
01:50:11.563 --> 01:50:15.740
And now there's a whole new generation of people,

2310
01:50:15.740 --> 01:50:17.041
they can do it for us.

2311
01:50:17.041 --> 01:50:20.330
We can be on the sidelines cheering them on.

2312
01:50:20.330 --> 01:50:22.590
So wait, so you're 55,

2313
01:50:22.590 --> 01:50:24.880
you just turned.
I just turned 55.

2314
01:50:24.880 --> 01:50:26.395
How's that feel it feel?

2315
01:50:26.395 --> 01:50:30.150
I mean, I feel good, I feel fine.

2316
01:50:30.150 --> 01:50:32.130
I think when I turned 50,

2317
01:50:32.130 --> 01:50:35.150
I didn't think twice about it, then when I turned 52,

2318
01:50:35.150 --> 01:50:37.490
like the white hair started coming in really fast

2319
01:50:37.490 --> 01:50:39.200
and I was like, oh, that's interesting.

2320
01:50:39.200 --> 01:50:41.010
Like this is different.

2321
01:50:41.010 --> 01:50:43.100
55, you're rounding towards 60.

2322
01:50:43.100 --> 01:50:45.520
So I think that one landed a little bit harder.

2323
01:50:45.520 --> 01:50:47.854
I don't know how it's been for you,

2324
01:50:47.854 --> 01:50:51.210
but well, I no spring chicken anymore,

2325
01:50:51.210 --> 01:50:53.653
but I get out and I get after it and I'm able to,

2326
01:50:53.653 --> 01:50:56.920
I wake up grateful every day that I feel good in my body

2327
01:50:56.920 --> 01:50:59.930
and I can go out and be active.

2328
01:50:59.930 --> 01:51:02.120
I got some back stuff and I'm not immune

2329
01:51:02.120 --> 01:51:04.090
from little things here and there

2330
01:51:04.090 --> 01:51:06.040
that are bugging me that didn't used to

2331
01:51:08.290 --> 01:51:09.123
One of the things I've learned

2332
01:51:09.123 --> 01:51:11.300
is nobody likes to really hear anybody complain

2333
01:51:11.300 --> 01:51:12.840
about aches and pains that they have.

2334
01:51:12.840 --> 01:51:15.760
It's kind of like a big yawner.

2335
01:51:15.760 --> 01:51:18.110
And you try and empathize with them.

2336
01:51:18.110 --> 01:51:20.897
But I had something happen to me

2337
01:51:20.897 --> 01:51:22.410
and it was one of the reasons why

2338
01:51:22.410 --> 01:51:25.690
last year was one of the most difficult in my life.

2339
01:51:25.690 --> 01:51:29.030
In addition to trying to launch this PLANTSTRONG brand,

2340
01:51:29.030 --> 01:51:33.810
and that is, I was on a mountain bike ride behind my house

2341
01:51:34.750 --> 01:51:36.493
and--
Try to forgot--

2342
01:51:36.493 --> 01:51:39.860
And I've been mountain biking for 30 years

2343
01:51:39.860 --> 01:51:44.600
and I'm like, Gumby, I fall and I'm like, okay,

2344
01:51:44.600 --> 01:51:48.030
took this fall and, oh my gosh.

2345
01:51:48.030 --> 01:51:51.290
I heard a snap, I got nauseous in my stomach.

2346
01:51:51.290 --> 01:51:54.020
I tried standing up, I fell back down on the ground.

2347
01:51:54.020 --> 01:51:57.086
I basically snapped my fibula.

2348
01:51:57.086 --> 01:51:59.350
And so I broke my ankle.

2349
01:51:59.350 --> 01:52:02.920
I had to be carried, carried out from the Greenbelt.

2350
01:52:02.920 --> 01:52:05.860
And I had surgery about a week later

2351
01:52:05.860 --> 01:52:09.710
and I had a plate and eight pins put on.

2352
01:52:09.710 --> 01:52:13.430
I didn't walk for almost five months.

2353
01:52:13.430 --> 01:52:18.410
Hobbling around on crutches or a little scooter.

2354
01:52:18.410 --> 01:52:21.830
It gave me such respect and empathy

2355
01:52:21.830 --> 01:52:23.620
for people that are handicapped,

2356
01:52:23.620 --> 01:52:27.520
that people have any issues with being ambulatory,

2357
01:52:27.520 --> 01:52:28.850
things like that.

2358
01:52:28.850 --> 01:52:31.570
I'm looking now for the handicap stalls,

2359
01:52:31.570 --> 01:52:35.630
the handicap ramp ways all that stuff.

2360
01:52:35.630 --> 01:52:36.730
That's rough man, five months.

2361
01:52:36.730 --> 01:52:38.090
I didn't realize--
Oh five months.

2362
01:52:38.090 --> 01:52:39.260
Five months.
Five months.

2363
01:52:39.260 --> 01:52:42.090
And I still have some issues

2364
01:52:42.090 --> 01:52:44.130
and I wanna have the plate in the pins taken out

2365
01:52:44.130 --> 01:52:46.467
because it just never feels quite right.

2366
01:52:46.467 --> 01:52:48.410
And I haven't really, I mean, I run,

2367
01:52:48.410 --> 01:52:50.130
but not like I used to.

2368
01:52:50.130 --> 01:52:51.510
You out on the bike at all though

2369
01:52:51.510 --> 01:52:52.760
or are you just hitting the pool?

2370
01:52:52.760 --> 01:52:54.180
No, no.
You're done.

2371
01:52:54.180 --> 01:52:57.066
No I'm biking, I got back on that horse

2372
01:52:57.066 --> 01:52:59.820
and it was scary the first week or so.

2373
01:52:59.820 --> 01:53:01.260
And the trails here,

2374
01:53:01.260 --> 01:53:04.380
have you run the Greenbelt at all?

2375
01:53:04.380 --> 01:53:09.380
Well, there's a lot of like corollary trails

2376
01:53:10.020 --> 01:53:11.820
that run off the main Greenbelt

2377
01:53:11.820 --> 01:53:13.900
and that's where the excitement is.

2378
01:53:13.900 --> 01:53:15.280
More like single track stuff.

2379
01:53:15.280 --> 01:53:17.860
Single track, ledges, roots rocks.

2380
01:53:17.860 --> 01:53:21.111
I mean, it it's gnarly stuff, you have to have some skills.

2381
01:53:21.111 --> 01:53:24.350
And so getting back on the bike was,

2382
01:53:24.350 --> 01:53:27.300
the first week was rough and then I got back,

2383
01:53:27.300 --> 01:53:30.210
but I'm always now a little bit more tentative

2384
01:53:30.210 --> 01:53:31.827
than I was before.

2385
01:53:31.827 --> 01:53:34.050
It's unavoidable, man.

2386
01:53:34.050 --> 01:53:36.640
But I'm glad you're back in one piece.

2387
01:53:36.640 --> 01:53:39.210
It feels good to be back.

2388
01:53:39.210 --> 01:53:44.210
And I just now just walking, I'm like, oh, thank you.

2389
01:53:46.190 --> 01:53:47.090
I'm so grateful.

2390
01:53:47.090 --> 01:53:51.770
But it made me think of our friend Paul de Gelder

2391
01:53:51.770 --> 01:53:56.770
and what that man went through and his spirit

2392
01:53:57.240 --> 01:53:59.283
and his tenacity.
That's unbelievable.

2393
01:54:00.640 --> 01:54:02.460
The manner in which he carries himself

2394
01:54:02.460 --> 01:54:05.780
with such gratitude and poise is remarkable.

2395
01:54:05.780 --> 01:54:07.180
I just saw him a couple of weeks ago.

2396
01:54:07.180 --> 01:54:09.450
I mean for people that don't know,

2397
01:54:09.450 --> 01:54:12.570
victim of a bull shark attack in Sydney Harbor

2398
01:54:12.570 --> 01:54:15.723
lost half his leg and half of one arm,

2399
01:54:17.310 --> 01:54:19.910
but has become this incredible ambassador

2400
01:54:19.910 --> 01:54:24.790
for shark preservation, for ecological responsibility.

2401
01:54:24.790 --> 01:54:27.450
And so many things, it's such a powerful voice.

2402
01:54:27.450 --> 01:54:29.270
And he goes into the gym and he kills it.

2403
01:54:29.270 --> 01:54:34.270
And just no excuses, no woe is me whatsoever.

2404
01:54:34.610 --> 01:54:37.580
Just like walks the talk.

2405
01:54:37.580 --> 01:54:38.904
Big time.

2406
01:54:38.904 --> 01:54:40.440
And he's now like the host of "Shark Week"

2407
01:54:40.440 --> 01:54:43.070
on Discovery Channel.
It's like a full-time job.

2408
01:54:43.070 --> 01:54:45.240
They got them flying all over the place all the time.

2409
01:54:45.240 --> 01:54:48.073
And doing stuff with like Mike Tyson.

2410
01:54:49.190 --> 01:54:52.020
He gets to see people like Mike Tyson and Will Smith

2411
01:54:52.020 --> 01:54:54.790
basically poop their pants.
I know.

2412
01:54:54.790 --> 01:54:55.973
It's hilarious, man.

2413
01:54:57.180 --> 01:54:59.960
We're not gonna end this without leaving people

2414
01:54:59.960 --> 01:55:01.960
with some takeaways

2415
01:55:01.960 --> 01:55:03.800
who are thinking about

2416
01:55:03.800 --> 01:55:06.550
getting on the plant strong bandwagon.

2417
01:55:06.550 --> 01:55:07.560
What do I do first?

2418
01:55:07.560 --> 01:55:09.160
How do I make this switch?

2419
01:55:09.160 --> 01:55:10.930
What do I watch out for?

2420
01:55:10.930 --> 01:55:12.800
Help me, Rip.

2421
01:55:12.800 --> 01:55:14.333
Help me help them.

2422
01:55:17.460 --> 01:55:19.470
Well, the first thing I would recommend

2423
01:55:19.470 --> 01:55:21.140
is you gotta start somewhere

2424
01:55:21.140 --> 01:55:24.510
and I would just go to plantstrong.com

2425
01:55:24.510 --> 01:55:28.370
and I would take the seven day challenge.

2426
01:55:28.370 --> 01:55:31.640
Literally anybody can do anything for seven days.

2427
01:55:31.640 --> 01:55:35.260
So take this challenge and don't think of it as a challenge

2428
01:55:35.260 --> 01:55:37.470
if you have a lot of challenges in your life,

2429
01:55:37.470 --> 01:55:41.336
think of it as an adventure and healthy eating.

2430
01:55:41.336 --> 01:55:46.336
And what I always tell people is just find foods

2431
01:55:47.690 --> 01:55:52.690
that you love and like do steel cut oats for breakfast,

2432
01:55:52.940 --> 01:55:56.253
with a slice banana, maybe a little bit of oat milk on top.

2433
01:55:57.890 --> 01:56:00.800
Do the commercialized, Rip's Big Bowl cereal, right?

2434
01:56:00.800 --> 01:56:03.193
Just anything.
You're a born marketer.

2435
01:56:04.810 --> 01:56:07.520
And then find a lunch

2436
01:56:07.520 --> 01:56:09.440
that you can like gravitate to,

2437
01:56:09.440 --> 01:56:11.180
like in that ice box right now,

2438
01:56:11.180 --> 01:56:15.650
I have got a sweet potato, right?

2439
01:56:15.650 --> 01:56:17.240
I've got frozen rice.

2440
01:56:18.300 --> 01:56:21.000
I've got a can of black beans, I got some salsa.

2441
01:56:21.000 --> 01:56:22.500
I'll just throw that together.

2442
01:56:23.740 --> 01:56:27.860
I always have some green leafies during the day,

2443
01:56:27.860 --> 01:56:29.430
preferably twice a day.

2444
01:56:29.430 --> 01:56:31.970
But my favorite dinner is always,

2445
01:56:31.970 --> 01:56:35.080
and we had last night rice and beans extravaganza,

2446
01:56:35.080 --> 01:56:38.610
just brown rice, Pinto beans, white beans, you name it.

2447
01:56:38.610 --> 01:56:41.060
And then I do a veggie relish on top

2448
01:56:41.060 --> 01:56:46.060
where it is sliced tomatoes, sliced up bell peppers,

2449
01:56:46.480 --> 01:56:50.020
a little bit of avocado, water, chestnuts,

2450
01:56:50.020 --> 01:56:51.313
little bit of tofu.

2451
01:56:52.360 --> 01:56:54.500
This lifestyle, I tell people,

2452
01:56:54.500 --> 01:56:58.270
this is the cheapest easiest way to eat

2453
01:56:58.270 --> 01:57:00.680
once you kind of get over yourself

2454
01:57:00.680 --> 01:57:01.890
and all the obstacles

2455
01:57:01.890 --> 01:57:04.670
that you're putting in front of yourself.

2456
01:57:04.670 --> 01:57:06.760
Again, I'm just gonna repeat really quickly.

2457
01:57:06.760 --> 01:57:10.970
Oatmeal, potatoes, rice and beans.

2458
01:57:10.970 --> 01:57:15.210
This is peasant food and it can be absolutely delicious

2459
01:57:15.210 --> 01:57:17.300
depending upon what you put on top of it.

2460
01:57:17.300 --> 01:57:18.810
I love putting Saracha

2461
01:57:18.810 --> 01:57:20.900
on a lot of my--
We go through

2462
01:57:20.900 --> 01:57:22.610
so many bottles of that stuff.

2463
01:57:22.610 --> 01:57:25.420
And then I'll go back to the community,

2464
01:57:25.420 --> 01:57:28.040
it's really important that you plug into a community

2465
01:57:28.040 --> 01:57:29.820
and you're getting support through a community

2466
01:57:29.820 --> 01:57:33.000
and we've got all kinds of free stuff,

2467
01:57:33.000 --> 01:57:34.940
community.plantstrong.com

2468
01:57:34.940 --> 01:57:39.940
with 25,000 people that are super active and engaged.

2469
01:57:40.650 --> 01:57:44.500
That just will be very welcoming and open you

2470
01:57:44.500 --> 01:57:46.730
or welcome you with open arms.

2471
01:57:46.730 --> 01:57:48.510
We've actually re-engaged

2472
01:57:48.510 --> 01:57:52.240
with our medical immersion programs.

2473
01:57:52.240 --> 01:57:55.360
So we did our first one in two years

2474
01:57:55.360 --> 01:57:58.167
because of COVID back in Sedona two weeks ago.

2475
01:57:58.167 --> 01:58:00.400
And we had 80 participants.

2476
01:58:00.400 --> 01:58:02.910
We had the whole plan strong crew that was there.

2477
01:58:02.910 --> 01:58:05.320
We had Dr. Michael clapper.

2478
01:58:05.320 --> 01:58:08.080
We had a guy named Dr. Brian Asbill

2479
01:58:08.080 --> 01:58:11.460
who's a rising cardiologists.
I don't know him.

2480
01:58:11.460 --> 01:58:13.996
He's a new guy on the block, he's amazing.

2481
01:58:13.996 --> 01:58:17.770
We had, of course my sister, my brother-in-law,

2482
01:58:17.770 --> 01:58:20.794
my parents did not make it to this one with,

2483
01:58:20.794 --> 01:58:23.820
with COVID and everything like that.

2484
01:58:23.820 --> 01:58:25.150
But we made sure it was safe when we,

2485
01:58:25.150 --> 01:58:28.060
everybody had to bring a test result

2486
01:58:28.060 --> 01:58:29.950
that they tested negative.

2487
01:58:29.950 --> 01:58:31.710
And so that was fantastic.

2488
01:58:31.710 --> 01:58:35.340
We're doing another one of those in Black Mountain

2489
01:58:35.340 --> 01:58:39.160
outside of Asheville, North Carolina in early March.

2490
01:58:39.160 --> 01:58:42.280
If anybody's interested in learning from soup to nuts

2491
01:58:42.280 --> 01:58:44.527
about the lifestyle, and we do,

2492
01:58:44.527 --> 01:58:49.527
Rich, we have bonfires at night, we have star gazing,

2493
01:58:49.970 --> 01:58:52.065
we have talent show night.

2494
01:58:52.065 --> 01:58:56.380
It's like food camp for kids.

2495
01:58:56.380 --> 01:59:00.080
And you learn to like find your inner child and laugh again

2496
01:59:00.080 --> 01:59:01.730
and have so much fun.

2497
01:59:01.730 --> 01:59:03.970
And I'm gonna deviate just for a sec

2498
01:59:03.970 --> 01:59:07.367
because something really powerful happened in Sedona

2499
01:59:07.367 --> 01:59:11.440
and I wanna try it on for size with you.

2500
01:59:11.440 --> 01:59:13.080
So one of the things we do

2501
01:59:13.080 --> 01:59:16.960
whenever we have a Sedona immersion is we have a big bonfire

2502
01:59:16.960 --> 01:59:21.230
and the Sedona's night sky is so spectacular,

2503
01:59:21.230 --> 01:59:22.170
and we have this bonfire

2504
01:59:22.170 --> 01:59:27.170
and we got all 80 people around it in chairs and it's cold.

2505
01:59:27.510 --> 01:59:30.663
And we just invite people to tell ghost stories.

2506
01:59:32.070 --> 01:59:35.070
And we typically have like 15 people

2507
01:59:35.070 --> 01:59:39.113
that decide to share and almost all of them are true.

2508
01:59:39.960 --> 01:59:42.593
And people will say how, you know what,

2509
01:59:44.020 --> 01:59:46.820
when I was a little girl, this is true,

2510
01:59:46.820 --> 01:59:48.808
when I was a little girl,

2511
01:59:48.808 --> 01:59:51.550
my mother had a best friend and she was beautiful

2512
01:59:51.550 --> 01:59:55.310
and she was an artist and we really connected.

2513
01:59:55.310 --> 01:59:58.400
And I would dream about her at night

2514
01:59:58.400 --> 02:00:00.350
that we'd be doing things

2515
02:00:00.350 --> 02:00:03.600
and she would be dreaming as well about us

2516
02:00:03.600 --> 02:00:04.530
doing things together.

2517
02:00:04.530 --> 02:00:06.570
And then when we'd meet, like in person,

2518
02:00:06.570 --> 02:00:07.420
we talk about,

2519
02:00:07.420 --> 02:00:09.490
oh, it wasn't that fun when we did all this together.

2520
02:00:09.490 --> 02:00:12.370
So they were visiting each other in their dreams.

2521
02:00:12.370 --> 02:00:15.480
And she said how she she's always had this ability

2522
02:00:15.480 --> 02:00:18.510
to kinda really connect with people, read people,

2523
02:00:18.510 --> 02:00:21.376
but as she's gotten older, she's kind of lost it.

2524
02:00:21.376 --> 02:00:25.400
Another woman got up and talked about how she,

2525
02:00:25.400 --> 02:00:27.330
when she was a little girl, there was this man,

2526
02:00:27.330 --> 02:00:28.600
the cookie man,

2527
02:00:28.600 --> 02:00:32.260
because he had this freshly made cookies in a cart

2528
02:00:32.260 --> 02:00:34.800
and he would come by their house in the neighborhood

2529
02:00:34.800 --> 02:00:36.900
and she'd always buy cookies from him.

2530
02:00:36.900 --> 02:00:40.438
And then she had a dream one night that the cookie man died.

2531
02:00:40.438 --> 02:00:43.010
And she found out the next morning from her mother

2532
02:00:43.010 --> 02:00:45.110
that the cookie man had died.

2533
02:00:45.110 --> 02:00:49.043
So she somehow was able to tap into that.

2534
02:00:50.690 --> 02:00:54.960
And it was like story and story again and again like that

2535
02:00:54.960 --> 02:00:58.730
and one of the stories that I told was one of my,

2536
02:00:58.730 --> 02:01:02.383
one of my wife's very good friends at a young age,

2537
02:01:03.250 --> 02:01:07.253
developed this ability to basically visit the dead.

2538
02:01:09.544 --> 02:01:12.070
And it sounds kind of crazy and far out

2539
02:01:12.070 --> 02:01:14.450
and listeners are probably--
I'm all about this.

2540
02:01:14.450 --> 02:01:15.450
Keep going.
Okay.

2541
02:01:18.900 --> 02:01:20.340
And she said, when she was a little girl,

2542
02:01:20.340 --> 02:01:25.330
what happened is something happened where her father died

2543
02:01:25.330 --> 02:01:28.820
and she was alone and she was scared

2544
02:01:28.820 --> 02:01:33.170
and this woman would come visit her at night

2545
02:01:33.170 --> 02:01:35.079
and hold her hand.

2546
02:01:35.079 --> 02:01:37.340
And this is started at age six.

2547
